---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Margin from '../components/Margin.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const essays = await getCollection('essays', ({ data }) => !data.draft);
const talks = await getCollection('speaking');
const media = await getCollection('media');
const projects = await getCollection('projects');

const n = (count: number, one: string, many = `${one}s`) => `${count} ${count === 1 ? one : many}`;

const earliest = essays.length
  ? essays.reduce((a, b) => (a.data.date < b.data.date ? a : b)).data.date.getUTCFullYear()
  : null;
---
<Base
  title="Colophon"
  description="What this site is made of: the type, the palette, the ship it is named after, and where the writing used to live."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="plate">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin
          label="Colophon"
          notes={[{ note: 'A colophon is the note at the end of a manuscript saying who made it and with what. It is the oldest form of build metadata.' }]}
        />
        <div>
          <h1>Sources</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            {n(essays.length, 'essay')}, {n(talks.length, 'talk')},
            {' '}{n(media.length, 'outside piece')} and {n(projects.length, 'project')}{earliest && `, the oldest of them from ${earliest}`}.
            Here is what they are made of.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin
          label="The name"
          notes={[{ note: 'Replace every plank and it is either the same ship or a very good copy. Two thousand years of argument, still unresolved.', tone: 'aside' }]}
        />
        <div class="body">
          <p>
            A xebec is a three-masted ship of the Mediterranean, fast and lateen-rigged and rebuilt
            constantly. It is also the vessel philosophers keep dismantling to ask whether a thing
            that has had all its parts replaced is still itself — which is the question this site is
            about, put in wood rather than in data.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin label="Type" />
        <div class="body plain">
          <p>
            <strong>Instrument Serif</strong> for display, <strong>Spectral</strong> for body text,
            both served from Google Fonts. Dates are set in old-style figures so the numerals sit
            into the line rather than shouting over it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin
          label="Colour"
          notes={[{ note: 'Three colours with a reason beats four with a mood.' }]}
        />
        <div class="body plain">
          <p>
            The palette is the portolan wind hierarchy, a convention of medieval sea charts: black
            for the eight principal winds, green for the eight half-winds, red for the sixteen
            quarter-winds. Red doubles as the active state and the drop cap. There is no fourth
            colour, deliberately.
          </p>
          <ul class="swatches">
            <li><span class="chip" style="background:var(--wind-principal)"></span> Principal winds</li>
            <li><span class="chip" style="background:var(--wind-half)"></span> Half-winds</li>
            <li><span class="chip" style="background:var(--wind-quarter)"></span> Quarter-winds</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin
          label="The rose"
          notes={[{ note: 'North is red because that is a navigational convention, not because red looked nice there.', tone: 'aside' }]}
        />
        <div class="body plain">
          <p>
            The compass rose computes its own geometry rather than being drawn: eight points, each
            split lengthwise into a filled and an outlined half so it reads as faceted brass instead
            of as a symbol. Its bezel carries the same thirty-two divisions as the bearing lines
            behind it, so the rose and the rhumbs are visibly one instrument.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin
          label="The margin"
          notes={[{ note: 'Like this one. It is never empty — that is the whole discipline of the layout.' }]}
        />
        <div class="body plain">
          <p>
            Every essay carries a margin column. Glosses are written inline in the prose as Obsidian
            callouts and lifted out at build time by a small remark plugin, which means a note is
            anchored to its paragraph rather than to a paragraph number that drifts the moment you
            insert a sentence. Below 880 pixels the margin moves above the text and the alignment
            script stands down.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin label="Machinery" />
        <div class="body plain">
          <p>
            <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a>, markdown in git, static hosting on
            <a href="https://pages.cloudflare.com">Cloudflare Pages</a>. No CMS, no database, no
            server: writing a post is a commit. Four content collections are schema-checked at build
            time, so a typo in a frontmatter field fails the build rather than rendering wrong.
            Comments, when they are switched on, are <a href="https://giscus.app">Giscus</a> threads
            living in GitHub Discussions — no trackers and no third-party cookies.
            The <a href="/rss.xml">feed</a> carries full text, not teasers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="grid">
        <Margin
          label="Provenance"
          notes={[{ note: 'An archive with holes in it is a worse thing than no archive, so the images came across too.', tone: 'aside' }]}
        />
        <div class="body plain">
          <p>
            This writing lived on Ghost at <a href="https://mikekiser.org">mikekiser.org</a> until
            2026. Everything moved across with its slug intact, images and all, and the old domain
            redirects here path for path — so a link made years ago still lands where it was aimed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Written and kept by {SITE.author} in {SITE.where}.
            <a href="/about/#contact">Corrections are welcome.</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .swatches { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1.4em 0 0; display: flex; gap: 26px; flex-wrap: wrap; font-size: 16.5px; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .swatches li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 9px; }
  .chip { display: inline-block; width: 15px; height: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
</style>

